Z||000040||DRH||02-13-2001||01:09 PM||||lou, <P>The difference is LI is doing it based entirely on published numbers for gear ratios and tire sizes. Tire sizes vary slightly by manufacturer and amount of wear, so there is a margin for error.<P>Your calculations assume the speedometer is correct when it shows 88 MPH. Given the inaccuracy of guages in general and Fiero guages in particular you also have a margin for error.<P>BOTH calculations are assuming the Tach is totally accurate. The difference in 140 and 149 is less than 300 RPM... probably well within the Fiero Tachs margin of error. <P>Like someone mentioned above... you're still haulin' a$$ any way you slice it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000041||lou_dias||02-13-2001||03:21 PM||||Yes, I see.<BR>Still can't wait to turbo it...||||reg||1
Z||000042||L.I. Fieros||02-13-2001||09:43 PM||||Here's the basic method I use for determining vehicle speed at a given engine RPM.  First I figure out what the circumference of the driving (rear) tires is.  This is found using the tire dimensions including the tire width, sidewall profile, and rim diameter.  You have 225/60/15's on your car which have a circumference of 6.71 feet.  Next I move on to the transmission and figure out what the speed of the output shaft (axles) is with respect to the input shaft (crankshaft) at the engine RPM in question.  For the Getrag 5 spd., the final drive ratio is 3.61 and the fifth gear ratio is 0.72    This multiplies out to an overall ratio of 2.60  Basically this means that the output shaft will rotate one time for every 2.6 revolutions of the input shaft.  So, if your engine is spinning at 4800 rpm, with no clutch slippage of course, then your wheels are rotating at 1847 rpm (4800/2.6).  With all this known we are ready to figure out what the vehicles speed is.  Just watch out for the units though.  Here's the final step:<P>(1847 wheel revs/min)*(6.71 ft/wheel rev)*(60 min/hr)*(1 mi/5280 ft)<P>This gives you 140.8 mph. at 4800 rpm.  This is in agreement with the 1.82% gain in tire circumference, which is directly related to the vehicle speed.  I tried to keep it short, oh well.<P>See ya,<BR>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000043||L.I. Fieros||02-13-2001||09:49 PM||||Oh, and DRH is definately right on.  There is PLENTY of room for error in all of this.  My calculations are purely theoretical.  That's probably why racing teams don't even bother with a speedo, just a huge tach.||||reg||1
